---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Navbar from "../components/Navbar.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

// Keep in step with version.json in this repo's root — that is what the in-app
// update check reads, and a page pointing at an older release than the manifest
// hands new users a build the app immediately wants to replace.
const version = "0.9.2";
const dmgURL = `https://github.com/Pratik-Singh-web/minnie-landing/releases/download/v${version}-beta/Minnie-${version}-beta.dmg`;
const expires = "7 November 2026";

const h2 = "prose-h2";
const p = "prose-p";
const ul = "prose-ul";
const link = "prose-a";
---

<Layout
  title="Download the Minnie beta — free"
  description="Download the free Minnie beta for Apple Silicon Macs. Minnie is a native macOS desktop pet that runs the AI coding agent you already use."
>
  <Navbar />

  <main class="legal">
    <a href={base} class="legal__back mono">← back to home</a>

    <h1 class="legal__h1">Download the beta</h1>
    <p class="legal__date mono">v{version} · free · expires {expires}</p>

    <p class={p} style="margin-top:28px;">
      Minnie is in open beta and free to use. She runs the AI coding agent you already have on
      your Mac, so there's no extra AI bill — but you do need your own agent.
    </p>

    <p style="margin:28px 0 12px;">
      <a href={dmgURL} class="btn">Download Minnie {version} →</a>
    </p>
    <p class="mono" style="opacity:.7;font-size:14px;">
      Apple Silicon · macOS 14+ · ~146 MB
    </p>

    <!-- The install block. This is the make-or-break section of the page: an
         unsigned app that needs a Terminal command is exactly where a curious
         person gives up. Hence the card treatment, the big numbered steps, and
         a copy button — the command is long and mistyping it fails silently.

         Do NOT promise a dialog-free install. macOS warns about unsigned apps in
         ways that vary by machine and OS version; telling someone they'll see no
         warning and then showing them one reads as "this app is broken", which is
         the exact impression this section exists to prevent. -->
    <h2 class={h2}>Installing</h2>

    <p class={p}>
      Takes about a minute. <strong>macOS will probably warn you about this app</strong> and
      you'll click past it once — Minnie isn't signed by Apple yet (a Developer ID costs
      $99/year; that comes with v1). Nothing about the warning is specific to Minnie; every
      unsigned Mac app gets it.
    </p>

    <div class="install card">
      <div class="install__head">
        <span class="mono">install · 3 steps</span>
      </div>

      <ol class="install__steps">
        <li>
          <span class="install__n mono">1</span>
          <div class="install__body">
            <p class="install__t">Open the disk image</p>
            <p class="install__d">
              Double-click <code>Minnie-{version}-beta.dmg</code> in your Downloads.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li>
          <span class="install__n mono">2</span>
          <div class="install__body">
            <p class="install__t">Paste this into Terminal</p>
            <p class="install__d">All three lines at once — they run in order.</p>
            <div class="install__codewrap">
              <button type="button" class="install__copy mono" id="copy-install">copy</button>
              <pre class="install__code mono"><code id="install-cmd">ditto --noextattr --norsrc /Volumes/Minnie*/Minnie.app /Applications/Minnie.app
xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/Minnie.app
open /Applications/Minnie.app</code></pre>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li>
          <span class="install__n mono">3</span>
          <div class="install__body">
            <p class="install__t">Look in your menu bar</p>
            <p class="install__d">
              Minnie has <strong>no Dock icon</strong> — that's deliberate. Find the paw at the
              top of your screen and click it to wake her.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div class="install__note">
        <p class="install__t">Still blocked?</p>
        <p class="install__d">
          If macOS says <em>"Apple could not verify Minnie is free of malware"</em>, open
          <strong>System Settings ▸ Privacy &amp; Security</strong>, scroll to the bottom, and
          click <strong>Open Anyway</strong> next to Minnie. You'll only ever do this once.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class={p}>
      Why the commands instead of dragging her to Applications? macOS marks the downloaded disk
      image as quarantined, and Finder copies that mark onto anything you drag out of it —
      which makes the warning stickier than it needs to be.
    </p>

    <h2 class={h2}>What you need</h2>
    <ul class={ul}>
      <li><strong>An Apple Silicon Mac</strong> (M1 or later). Intel Macs aren't supported yet.</li>
      <li><strong>macOS 14 Sonoma</strong> or later.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your own AI coding agent</strong> — Claude Code, Gemini CLI, Codex, or a custom
        command. Minnie drives the agent you already pay for; she doesn't resell AI.
      </li>
      <li>A microphone, if you want to talk to her. Typing works fine too.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class={h2}>Beta caveats</h2>
    <ul class={ul}>
      <li>Things will break. That's the point — tell me when they do.</li>
      <li>
        Minnie can run real commands on your Mac. She asks before each one; read what you
        approve.
      </li>
      <li>
        This build stops working on <strong>{expires}</strong>. She checks for new versions on
        launch and can update herself, so you shouldn't have to come back here.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class={p} style="margin-top:28px;">
      Something broken, confusing, or delightful?
      <a href="mailto:pratiksingh30701@gmail.com" class={link}>Email me</a> — beta feedback goes
      straight to the person who wrote it.
    </p>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  /* Install card — deliberately the loudest thing on the page after the download
     button. A Terminal command is where a curious person bails, so it gets a
     border, a banded header and numbered steps rather than a plain <ol>. */
  .install {
    margin: 20px 0 28px;
    padding: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .install__head {
    background: var(--yellow);
    color: #101a12;
    border-bottom: 3px solid var(--line);
    padding: 9px 18px;
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .install__steps {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 6px 0;
  }
  .install__steps > li {
    display: flex;
    gap: 14px;
    padding: 16px 18px;
    align-items: flex-start;
  }
  .install__steps > li + li {
    border-top: 2px dashed var(--line);
  }
  .install__n {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 30px;
    height: 30px;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    border: 3px solid var(--line);
    background: var(--green);
    color: var(--on-green);
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 800;
    box-shadow: var(--sh-sm);
  }
  .install__body { min-width: 0; flex: 1 1 auto; }
  .install__t {
    margin: 2px 0 4px;
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 17px;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .install__d {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink-dim);
    line-height: 1.55;
  }
  .install__codewrap { position: relative; margin-top: 10px; }
  .install__code {
    /* Must scroll INSIDE its own box — a long command that widens the page
       gives every phone a horizontal scrollbar. */
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 14px 16px;
    border: 3px solid var(--line);
    /* Fixed terminal palette, matching .hero__term. NOT var(--band): that flips
       from dark green to yellow between themes, which made the command block
       shout as loudly as the yellow header sitting right above it. */
    background: var(--green-deep);
    color: #cfe6d8;
    font-size: 12.5px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    box-shadow: var(--sh-sm);
  }
  .install__copy {
    position: absolute;
    top: 8px;
    right: 8px;
    z-index: 1;
    cursor: pointer;
    border: 2px solid var(--line);
    background: var(--card);
    color: var(--ink);
    padding: 4px 10px;
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 800;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .install__copy:hover { background: var(--yellow); color: #101a12; }
  .install__note {
    border-top: 3px solid var(--line);
    border-left: 8px solid var(--pink);
    background: var(--card);
    padding: 16px 18px;
  }

  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .install__steps > li { padding: 14px; gap: 10px; }
    .install__code { font-size: 11px; }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // The command is three lines and easy to mistype; a wrong path fails silently.
  const btn = document.getElementById("copy-install");
  const src = document.getElementById("install-cmd");
  btn?.addEventListener("click", async () => {
    try {
      await navigator.clipboard.writeText(src?.textContent?.trim() ?? "");
      btn.textContent = "copied ✓";
      setTimeout(() => (btn.textContent = "copy"), 1600);
    } catch {
      btn.textContent = "select it";
      setTimeout(() => (btn.textContent = "copy"), 1600);
    }
  });
</script>
